# Implement read-only mailbox workflows

## Goal

Ship useful read access before any mailbox mutation is enabled.

## Required work

- Implement mailbox and label listing.
- Implement paginated message and thread search.
- Implement message, header, and attachment retrieval.
- Implement conversation retrieval with explicit mapping between Proton
  conversations and provider-neutral threads.
- Bound body and attachment sizes and sanitize filenames.
- Preserve stable identifiers across pagination and reconnects.

## Current evidence

- Mailbox/label catalog metadata can be captured passively and retained as
  immutable in-memory snapshots, then paged with authenticated
  account/generation/query-bound cursors without rereading Proton during
  continuation. Public transport DTO fields remain undefined by the frozen
  contract, so no wire shape is invented.
- Exact single-message `mail/v4/messages/{messageID}` responses can now be
  reduced to bounded identity/envelope/read-state/label/MIME and attachment
  descriptor metadata while dropping body, headers, passwords, cryptographic
  packets, and attachment bytes. The managed browser can passively wait for the
  next app-owned detail GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or
  injecting
  a provider call, with clean timeout and ambiguity handling. This still does
  not satisfy targeted `get_message`: current WebClients may use cached message
  body without a network fetch, and plaintext body/header plus attachment
  decryption evidence remains open.

- Provider-neutral attachment output now has pure safety primitives before any
  filesystem access. Untrusted provider filenames are reduced to one portable
  UTF-8 leaf capped at 255 bytes, path separators/control characters are
  neutralized, leading-dot and Windows device-name hazards are removed, and
  diagnostics redact the resulting filename.
- Caller attachment destinations normalize only when strictly relative and
  reject parent traversal, absolute roots, backslashes, control characters, and
  drive/alternate-stream colon syntax. Inline eligibility uses the frozen
  `32 KiB` contract ceiling exactly.
- The local runtime now owns bounded file output under an explicit absolute
  root. It opens every root/destination directory component through held file
  descriptors with no-follow semantics, enforces the configured byte ceiling
  before creating output, writes a private `0600` `.partial` inode, and commits
  with an atomic hard link that never overwrites an existing entry. Synthetic
  tests cover nested output, root and destination symlinks, later root-path
  replacement, existing file/symlink refusal, missing parents, oversized bytes,
  private mode, single-link cleanup, and redacted diagnostics. This boundary is
  not yet wired to a public attachment tool because plaintext browser retrieval
  remains unresolved.

- Exact attachment metadata responses now have a separate bounded projector for
  `GET /api/mail/v4/attachments/{attachment_id}/metadata`. It requires provider
  success, binds body `Attachment.ID` to the requested attachment, and binds
  `Attachment.MessageID` to the caller-expected parent message before retaining
  only sanitized filename, declared size, and MIME type. A target-session CDP
  classifier tracks only exact metadata GET lifecycles, accepts only HTTP-200
  JSON with stable attachment identity, discards failed app retries, and caps
  unfinished matching requests at 16. Sender/address data, conversation
  identity, key packets, signatures, request headers, and cookies are discarded.
- The managed browser can now wait passively for the next app-owned attachment
  metadata GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or injecting a provider
  request. It bounds the wait to 30 seconds, enables Network with a 256 KiB
  resource and 1 MiB total buffer, projects only the completed exact metadata
  response, and always disables Network before returning success, timeout, or
  projection failure. A response must still bind to the caller-expected parent
  message, concurrent/residual matching requests fail as ambiguous, and a clean
  timeout is `None`. Synthetic browser tests cover success, no-action timeout,
  parent mismatch cleanup, and a request starting during Network teardown.
- Current WebClients fetches encrypted attachment bytes and full metadata in
  parallel, derives the attachment session key, then calls `decryptAndVerify`
  client-side. Therefore the metadata projector proves message-scoped identity
  and safe metadata only; it does not make encrypted HTTP bytes equivalent to
  the plaintext `attachment_v1` response.

- Exact unparameterized conversation responses now have a separate bounded
  membership projector and target-session Network classifier for
  `GET /api/mail/v4/conversations/{conversation_id}`. It requires provider
  success, exact response/request conversation identity, a bounded
  `Conversation.NumMessages`, `Messages.length == NumMessages`, unique message
  IDs, and every member `ConversationID` matching the parent. Only member ID and
  numeric `Time` survive, in provider response order; bodies, headers, subject,
  recipients, attachments, and cryptographic fields are discarded.
- The classifier rejects `?MessageID=...` conversation requests because their
  slicing/completeness semantics are not yet proven. This establishes exact
  provider thread membership for one complete response, not public
  `get_thread`: plaintext members still require independently bounded reads and
  the provider-neutral ordered thread mapping remains unwired.
- The managed browser can now wait passively for the next complete app-owned
  unparameterized conversation GET without clicking, navigating, reloading, or
  injecting a provider request. It bounds the wait to 30 seconds, uses bounded
  Network buffers, projects only the completed exact response, and disables
  Network before success, clean timeout, or projection failure. Residual or late
  exact conversation requests fail as ambiguous. Synthetic browser tests cover
  complete success, no-action timeout, incomplete membership cleanup, and a
  request starting during Network teardown.
- Conversation list/search responses now have a separate bounded projector and
  exact-endpoint Network classifier. Fresh responses require `Stale=0`, no
  active `TasksRunning`, bounded `Total`, at most 100 unique conversations, and
  numeric `Time`, provider `Order`, and `NumMessages`; only those fields plus
  opaque conversation ID survive. Request/response URL identity is bound with
  randomized in-memory fingerprints, so search terms and query text are not
  retained. This intentionally does not interpret Page/Anchor continuation or
  claim snapshot/order compatibility for public `list_threads`/search; those
  provider-neutral semantics remain separate work.
- The conversation-list response projector can now match stable visible row
  IDs to one already-observed response, returning only ID, numeric Time,
  provider Order, and message count in rendered order. Missing/duplicated
  visible IDs, excessive rows, and contradictory empty-page evidence fail
  closed. It does not by itself identify conversation render mode, tie a
  request to an immutable page, or permit public list/search operations.
- The managed browser now offers an explicitly passive, bounded wait for the
  next app-owned exact conversation-list GET. It checks ready Mail shell and
  origin before/after the capture, disables Network on success, timeout, or
  projection failure, and rejects residual concurrent requests. Synthetic
  Chromium-pipe tests cover successful fresh projection, clean timeout, stale
  response cleanup, and a request starting during Network teardown. No
  provider-neutral thread search, pagination, or immutable snapshot claim is
  implied.

- A new managed-browser diagnostic couples those bounded list metadata rows
  with a same-session content-free before/after visible list. It blocks known
  message-only routes, loading, changed row IDs or pagination, and absent
  provider IDs. It never navigates or fetches provider data itself. This does
  not bind a Mail route to a cursor, prove conversation mode when unknown, or
  implement public `list_threads`.

- Conversation-list GET captures now retain sanitized request page and anchor
  presence, never full query text. The visible-ID diagnostic rejects missing
  or inconsistent request page numbers and anchor continuations, and does not
  treat a bare ID match as sufficient page evidence. This is deliberately
  narrower than provider-neutral paginated list/search and carries no server
  snapshot consistency guarantee.

- Explicitly empty visible conversation evidence now requires both an empty
  provider row set and `Total=0`. A syntactically fresh response with zero
  returned rows but positive provider total is treated as contradictory,
  without inferring that a later page represents an empty mailbox.

- For visible-ID diagnostics, sanitized conversation-list page/anchor
  evidence is now checked immediately after the app-owned GET lifecycle and
  *before* requesting its response body. Requests for another page or an
  anchor continuation fail without reading provider JSON at all; Network is
  still disabled before surfacing the error. The independent, explicitly
  passive raw-list observer retains its previous behavior.

- The passive conversation-list classifier now rejects contradictory numeric
  paging metadata (`PageSize != Limit`) and a numbered `Page` mixed with
  `Anchor`/`AnchorID`. These are separate initial-versus-continuation shapes
  in the reviewed WebClients request path; rejecting their mixture prevents
  a false positive page match without interpreting any search/label text.

- A consumed conversation-list CDP request ID remains terminal until the
  observation ends. Bounded content-free completion markers prevent a late
  replay/failure after the original request has been handed off from silently
  bypassing lifecycle checks. This does not establish immutable result paging
  or enable public thread search.

- The same bounded terminal-ID protection now covers complete conversation,
  single-message detail, and attachment-metadata GET captures. Consumption
  retains only the CDP request ID (not content), so late lifecycle events
  cannot be mistaken for unrelated background traffic. Synthetic fake-browser
  replay tests exercise each diagnostic without new provider action authority.

- The visible-message batch Network classifier now keeps up to 128 consumed
  request IDs as terminal, preserving request-context checks while detecting
  late replies or failures during teardown. A synthetic reload/disable replay
  proves subsequent safe browser inspection still works. This strengthens
  diagnostics without changing public read/search availability.

- CDP request-ID retention is now bounded to 512 bytes across message-list,
  message-detail, conversation-detail, attachment-metadata, and conversation-
  list reads. This applies only to exact app-owned GET shapes, not other
  background traffic; empty/oversized matching IDs fail closed without being
  persisted into capture state or debug diagnostics.

- Visible conversation metadata now requires both PageSize and Limit to
  agree alongside a numeric initial Page, not merely a matching page number.
  Missing initial-request shape fails before `Network.getResponseBody` and
  does not affect the independent passive diagnostic; this still does not
  create snapshot isolation or public thread-list support.

- Visible conversation reconciliation additionally refuses `BeginID`,
  `EndID`, and `ID` selectors, because these need not describe the currently
  rendered page even when Page/PageSize/Limit agree. The presence-only flag is
  kept internally; selector values and private query text are discarded.

- Mailbox/label catalog capture now retains consumed CDP request IDs until
  observation teardown. A bounded 16-request ledger and 512-byte ID ceiling
  prevent late lifecycle replays and unbounded diagnostics; unrelated contact
  groups remain ignored. Synthetic browser tests verify failure recovery and
  safe subsequent Mail-shell inspection.

- Network lifecycle observers now reject late loading failures on already
  finished but unconsumed catalog, message-detail, conversation-detail,
  attachment-metadata, and event GETs. This closes the gap between receipt and
  consumption of a safe response; actual pre-completion app failures continue
  to permit app-owned retries without additional adapter provider requests.

- Exact detail GET identities now refuse backslash characters as opaque URL
  path separators for messages, conversations, and attachment metadata.
  Synthetic CDP requests prove those ambiguous paths fail closed before any
  response body can be read or mapped to provider-neutral identifiers.

- Message-list CDP request URLs are now bounded to 16 KiB before hashing
  untrusted query context, with the same bound enforced on matching responses.
  Message and conversation list URL classifiers also refuse fragments as
  non-network identities. This prevents synthetic fragment/query drift from
  being mistaken for app-owned list evidence without inventing public paging.

- The bounded message-list query fingerprint now preserves the relative
  order of repeated keys while canonicalizing distinct query-key placement.
  This is a conservative identity check rather than provider search semantics;
  unknown repeated filter values cannot reorder silently between batches.

- Direct list response projection now enforces the capture's 16 KiB URL
  input ceiling for both messages and conversations; even standalone parser
  calls cannot interpret an overlong exact list endpoint as safe metadata.

- Message-list and conversation-list observation now agree with the other
  read captures: `loadingFailed` after `loadingFinished` is a lifecycle
  contradiction, even before the completed request has been consumed. Pure
  synthetic tests prove the completed request remains recoverable for cleanup.

- Projected message and conversation list IDs now use the same bounded
  route-component safety rules as their detail-read endpoints, additionally
  disallowing Unicode whitespace and controls. Rendered-row reconciliation
  fails before metadata lookup on such IDs; provider content never enters
  an error or debug diagnostic. This is still not public paginated reading.

- Complete conversation membership now rejects unsafe member-message and
  parent identifiers as well as unsafe exact detail URL components, aligning
  its projected IDs with bounded list-read route safety. Only synthetic
  identity metadata is retained; plaintext thread reading remains blocked.

- Message-detail projection now rejects malformed exact message URL IDs,
  unsafe parent conversation identifiers, label IDs, and attachment descriptor
  IDs, while preserving content-bearing metadata and valid printable IDs.
  This remains bounded metadata only; body decryption and headers are not read.

- Attachment metadata projection now validates both attachment and expected
  parent-message IDs as bounded, safe opaque route components before reading
  bodies. Invalid caller identities or provider body fields fail closed;
  filename and MIME processing remain independent of identity safety.

- System folder, custom folder, and label catalog IDs now enforce safe
  opaque identifier rules independently of their user-defined display names.
  Nested `ParentID` values receive the same checks; safe printable IDs and
  non-route display text continue to project without changing public tools.

- Message-list continuation anchors are now checked as bounded safe opaque
  IDs rather than length-only strings; malformed separators and Unicode
  whitespace fail before a continuation can become list evidence.

- Visible mailbox-list inspection now enforces safe opaque row IDs before
  content-bearing page snapshots can inherit them. Synthetic browser tests
  reject whitespace and separator-bearing DOM attributes in the earliest
  content-free list read, rather than relying on later network reconciliation.

- Every route-bound read identifier now rejects the special path segments
  `.` and `..`, in addition to whitespace, controls, and delimiters. Synthetic
  list, detail, catalog and visible-page evidence closes path normalization
  ambiguity before provider-neutral read or cursor processing.

- The read-only adapter now centralizes route-bound opaque-ID validation in
  a private, content-free module shared by list, message/conversation detail,
  attachment metadata, catalog, event, and rendered-page evidence. This is
  an internal defensive boundary, not a newly advertised public read feature.

- Percent-escaped path components and selected invisible Unicode format
  controls now fail the shared read-ID predicate before list, detail,
  attachment, catalog and visible-row identity can be exposed. The validator
  never decodes or normalizes provider IDs; user-visible names and content
  fields retain their independent bounds.

- Exact conversation-list response URLs are bounded at 16 KiB before any
  private request-correlation hash is computed, matching the request limit.
  Synthetic boundary checks prove the largest accepted URL and fail closed
  on an oversized response without producing list metadata.

- Conversation-list continuation parsing now refuses malformed or
  overlong `AnchorID` values and non-decimal/overflowing numeric `Anchor`
  timestamps. This prevents unsafe request-shape evidence from reaching a
  visible-page diagnostic, while leaving full cursor/snapshot proof open.

- Message-list numeric pagination selectors now require canonical bounded
  ASCII-decimal values, removing alternate encodings of page, size, direction,
  and time anchors before any visible-list request can be correlated.

- Conversation-list page and size selectors now use the same decimal-only
  shape policy as the message list, refusing ambiguous signed or encoded
  numbers before a browser page may be associated with a Network response.

- Exact label/catalog `Type` selectors now reject alternate numeric
  representations, so a decoded catalog cannot be attributed to a loosely
  parsed category. Unsupported canonical numeric types remain unobserved.

- Numeric list page and continuation shape validators now reject
  leading-zero aliases, preventing noncanonical URLs from matching the same
  rendered page or continuation timestamp during passive request capture.

- Content-free visible pagination indicators now use the same canonical
  numeric page representation as Network list requests, so a noncanonical
  DOM page marker cannot be reconciled with a different app-owned GET.

- The full custom-folder catalog projector now verifies that references
  among captured folders do not form cycles. It preserves external/root
  parent identifiers without assuming that all parent IDs name catalog rows.

- Visible pagination now rejects an enabled successor from page `u32::MAX`
  before the read-only adapter can attempt impossible numeric page advancement.

- Passive message-list metadata now honors an explicitly unsuccessful
  provider `Code` rather than accepting fresh-looking rows from it. Missing
  optional `Code` remains compatible pending verified wire-schema evidence;
  browser tests cover the typed rejection without exposing message content.

- Message metadata reconciliation now checks provider batch totals across
  one visible-page read and rejects `Total>0` when the rendered page is
  explicitly empty. This closes a mismatch with the existing conversation
  diagnostic without treating equal totals as a snapshot guarantee.

- Network-driven message-list batch collection now compares provider
  totals before emitting the captured diagnostic. A changed total fails as
  incompatible instead of reaching metadata reconciliation with mixed state.

- Header research found two distinct fields on the exact message-detail
  response. `Message.Header` is raw header text, while `Message.ParsedHeaders`
  maps names to either one string or an array of strings; WebClients reads the
  parsed form for message semantics and combines raw `Header` with
  MIME-generated
  headers when exporting a message. The frozen contract has no standalone
  `get_headers` capability and only says `message_v1` contains headers requested
  by the caller. Its transport DTO/header-selection and normalization shape is
  still undefined, so the adapter continues to discard both fields rather than
  inventing casing, duplicate-value ordering, or raw-versus-parsed semantics.

- Rendered-body research confirms the opened message article exposes the
  provider
  ID through `data-message-id` and its same-origin content iframe exposes
  `#proton-root`. That is not yet a complete message-body source: WebClients can
  remove collapsed quoted content from the iframe DOM, applies renderer/search
  transforms, and marks unread messages read after expansion/body load. A future
  body projection must remain passive and prove completeness before mapping this
  surface to provider-neutral message body semantics.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every path is classified read-only by the tool contract.
- Search, pagination, Unicode, MIME, and malformed-message cases are tested.
- Attachment traversal and oversized outputs fail safely.
- Logs contain identifiers and timing only, never content.
- The primary web UI adapter passes every read workflow; no paid Bridge or
  direct-API client identity is an unstated prerequisite.

## Evidence

- Synthetic mailbox fixtures
- Contract and adapter integration tests
